      <article>
        <p class="lead"><strong>The Best Answer Hub GPA Calculator is a free, browser-based tool that works out your weighted, unweighted, and cumulative GPA across 8 grading scales, and shows the working so you can trust the number.</strong> This guide explains how GPA is actually calculated, the difference between weighted and unweighted, how to combine terms into a cumulative GPA, how to plan the grades you need to hit a target, and how international grades map onto the US 4.0 scale.</p>

        <div class="cav">Start here</div>
        <h2 id="what">What is the Best Answer Hub GPA Calculator?</h2>
        <p>The Best Answer Hub <a href="/calculators/gpa-calculator/">GPA Calculator</a> is a single-page tool that turns your grades and credit hours into a grade point average, on whichever scale your school uses. It handles the US 4.0 and 5.0 scales, percentage grades, UK Honours, the German system, the Indian CGPA, and the Australian scale, and it reports weighted and unweighted GPA together with a running cumulative figure. A target planner shows the grades needed to reach a goal, and every result comes with step-by-step working you can check. Like the rest of Best Answer Hub, it runs entirely in your browser with no account and no ads, so your grades stay on your device, and it stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising. The tool lives in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/calculators/">Calculators hub</a> and is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>.</p>

        <div class="cav">The real formula</div>
        <h2 id="how">How do you calculate your GPA?</h2>
        <p>You calculate a GPA by turning each grade into grade points, multiplying by the course credit hours, adding those up, and dividing by the total credit hours, and the Best Answer Hub GPA Calculator runs every step of that for you. It is a credit-weighted average, not a simple average of your grades, which is the single most common mistake. On the US 4.0 scale an A is 4.0, a B is 3.0, a C is 2.0, and a D is 1.0, with an F worth nothing (<a href="https://bigfuture.collegeboard.org/plan-for-college/get-started/how-to-calculate-gpa-4.0-scale" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">College Board</a>). Plus and minus grades shift the value by about 0.3, so an A- is roughly 3.7 and a B+ about 3.3, though the exact decimals vary by school; the University of Illinois, for instance, scores a B+ as 3.33 (<a href="https://registrar.illinois.edu/courses-grades/calculate-your-gpa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Illinois Registrar</a>). To find the GPA, multiply each course grade points by its credit hours to get quality points, sum the quality points, and divide by the total credit hours (<a href="https://registrar.arizona.edu/records-enrollment/enrollment/grades/grades-and-grade-point-average-gpa-calculation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Arizona</a>).</p>
        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption class="chart-title">The US 4.0 grade-point scale</figcaption>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 600 280" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of US 4.0 grade points: A equals 4.0, A minus 3.7, B plus 3.3, B 3.0, C 2.0, D 1.0, F 0.0">
            <defs><linearGradient id="bargrad" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1"><stop offset="0" stop-color="#C6603F"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#8a4429"/></linearGradient></defs>
            <line x1="52" y1="232" x2="565" y2="232" stroke="#E1D4be" stroke-width="2"/>
            <rect x="70" y="62" width="44" height="170" rx="7" fill="url(#bargrad)"/>
            <text x="92" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">4.0</text>
            <text x="92" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#847B6B">A</text>
            <rect x="140" y="75" width="44" height="157" rx="7" fill="url(#bargrad)"/>
            <text x="162" y="65" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">3.7</text>
            <text x="162" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#847B6B">A-</text>
            <rect x="210" y="92" width="44" height="140" rx="7" fill="url(#bargrad)"/>
            <text x="232" y="82" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">3.3</text>
            <text x="232" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#847B6B">B+</text>
            <rect x="280" y="104" width="44" height="128" rx="7" fill="url(#bargrad)"/>
            <text x="302" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">3.0</text>
            <text x="302" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#847B6B">B</text>
            <rect x="350" y="147" width="44" height="85" rx="7" fill="url(#bargrad)" opacity="0.82"/>
            <text x="372" y="137" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">2.0</text>
            <text x="372" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#847B6B">C</text>
            <rect x="420" y="189" width="44" height="43" rx="7" fill="url(#bargrad)" opacity="0.66"/>
            <text x="442" y="179" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">1.0</text>
            <text x="442" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#847B6B">D</text>
            <rect x="490" y="226" width="44" height="6" rx="3" fill="url(#bargrad)" opacity="0.5"/>
            <text x="512" y="216" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">0.0</text>
            <text x="512" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#847B6B">F</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="chart-src">US 4.0 scale grade points (College Board), with the plus and minus values used by the University of Illinois. Exact plus and minus decimals vary by school.</p>
        </figure>
        <p>This is why you cannot simply average your term GPAs. Suppose a light term of 3.9 across 9 credits is followed by a heavier term of 3.2 across 21 credits. Averaging the two numbers gives 3.55, but the correct credit-weighted result is 3.41, because the 21-credit term carries more than twice the weight. The Best Answer Hub calculator does this weighting automatically and shows each step, so the number you get is the one a registrar would.</p>

        <div class="cav">The big confusion</div>
        <h2 id="weighted">What is the difference between weighted and unweighted GPA?</h2>
        <p>An unweighted GPA scores every course on the same 4.0 scale, while a weighted GPA gives extra points to harder classes like honors, AP, or IB, which is why a weighted GPA can climb above 4.0. The Best Answer Hub GPA Calculator reports both figures at once, so you always know which one you are reading. The College Board describes weighting as assigning higher grade points to advanced courses, and notes that schools do it differently, so two students with identical report cards can show different weighted GPAs (<a href="https://bigfuture.collegeboard.org/plan-for-college/get-started/how-to-calculate-gpa-4.0-scale" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">College Board</a>). There is no single national rule: a common pattern adds 0.5 for honors and 1.0 for AP, but that varies from school to school. This is the source of the worry students raise most: the weighted number a high school prints is often not the number a college uses. Many universities recalculate applicants onto a standard unweighted scale, sometimes counting only core academic courses, to compare everyone fairly. The Best Answer Hub calculator shows weighted and unweighted side by side and lets you set the honors and AP bump, so you can reproduce either view rather than guess.</p>
        <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">The number that follows you</div><p>Because weighting is not standardized, treat the weighted GPA your school reports as one view, not the only one. When you compare yourself to a college average or a scholarship cutoff, check which GPA it refers to. Seeing both figures at once is the fastest way to avoid comparing a weighted number against an unweighted target.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Rolling it up</div>
        <h2 id="cumulative">How do you calculate cumulative GPA across terms?</h2>
        <p>Your cumulative GPA is the credit-weighted average of every course across all your terms, not the average of your term GPAs, and the Best Answer Hub calculator keeps that running total for you as you add each term. A term GPA covers one semester; a cumulative GPA rolls up your whole record (<a href="https://registrar.arizona.edu/records-enrollment/enrollment/grades/grades-and-grade-point-average-gpa-calculation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Arizona</a>). To combine terms correctly, add the quality points from every course in every term and divide by the total credit hours, exactly as you would for a single term. Pass or fail courses and transferred credits are usually left out, though policies differ by school, and combining two schools works the same way when a program accepts the credits. That credit-weighting is easy to get wrong by hand, which is why the Best Answer Hub calculator shows the quality points and totals behind every cumulative figure it reports.</p>

        <div class="cav">Plan the climb</div>
        <h2 id="target">What grades do I need to reach my target GPA?</h2>
        <p>To reach a target GPA, you need enough quality points across your remaining credits to pull the cumulative average up to your goal, and the Best Answer Hub target planner works out exactly what that takes. The more credits you have already banked, the harder each point is to move. This is the question students ask most: can I get from a 2.0 to a 3.0, or raise a 3.4 to a 3.6 before applying. The answer depends on how many credits you have already earned versus how many are left, because a strong final year moves a small record a long way and a large record only a little. It works like this: enter your current GPA, credits completed, and credits remaining, and the planner shows the average grade you need across what is left, or tells you honestly when a goal is out of reach. The same tool answers the reverse question, how far a single low grade will pull you down, so you can see the real cost of one hard class before you panic.</p>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Why later grades move the needle less</div><p>A 4.0 semester lifts a 12-credit record far more than a 120-credit one, because the new grades are a small share of a large total. That is not a reason to give up, but it is a reason to plan early. The planner shows exactly how much room is left, so a goal is set on math rather than hope.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Across borders</div>
        <h2 id="international">How do international grades convert to a US GPA?</h2>
        <p>International grades convert to a US 4.0 GPA only approximately, because each country grades differently and each university applies its own mapping, and the Best Answer Hub calculator handles the main systems with the mapping shown so the estimate stays transparent. A UK upper-second (2:1), an Indian CGPA out of 10, a German grade where 1.0 is best, and the Australian scale each translate into a US GPA range rather than a single exact figure. The Best Answer Hub calculator includes presets for the US 4.0 and 5.0 scales, percentage, UK Honours, German, Indian CGPA, and Australian systems. It is a planning aid, not an official evaluation: admissions offices and services such as <a href="https://www.wes.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WES</a> apply their own formulas, and a formal course-by-course evaluation can cost around USD 205, so for applications you should usually report your original grades and let the institution convert them.</p>

        <div class="cav">The honest comparison</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is it different from other GPA calculators?</h2>
        <p>The difference is breadth in one place: the Best Answer Hub GPA Calculator combines every scale, weighting, cumulative tracking, and target planning on a single page with no signup, where most free calculators do one job on one scale, often on an ad-supported page. The table sets the usual experience next to this one.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you get</th><th>Best Answer Hub</th><th>Typical GPA calculator</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Grading scales</td><td class="band">8, including international</td><td>Usually US 4.0 only</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weighted (AP/honors)</td><td class="band">Yes, adjustable</td><td>Sometimes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cumulative across terms</td><td class="band">Yes</td><td>Sometimes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Target-GPA planner</td><td class="band">Yes</td><td>Rarely</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Account or signup</td><td class="band">Not required</td><td>Sometimes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ads</td><td class="band">None</td><td>Common</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Where it runs</td><td class="band">In your browser</td><td>Varies</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Popular calculators tend to specialize. Some handle only the US 4.0 scale, some add per-course AP and honors weighting but no international scales, and others cover international conversion but split it across many separate pages, several on ad-supported sites. The Best Answer Hub GPA Calculator brings the common jobs together: 8 scales, weighted and unweighted, cumulative tracking, a target planner, plus and minus grades, step-by-step working, and a PDF export, all free and in the browser.</p>

        <div class="cta">
          <span class="kicker">Work out your GPA</span>
